84
00:03:03,460 --> 00:03:05,640
I mean, then I would go and have a policy

85
00:03:05,640 --> 00:03:06,660
with no deductible all the time.

86
00:03:07,020 --> 00:03:08,720
But you want me to go and get a deductible

87
00:03:08,880 --> 00:03:11,880
for the next three days on the chance that I might have

88
00:03:11,880 --> 00:03:13,740
to pay $500 while driving this car.

89
00:03:14,040 --> 00:03:15,100
Exactly. Right?

90
00:03:15,100 --> 00:03:17,780
And this, they use the sales technique all the time

91
00:03:17,780 --> 00:03:18,740
in my experience.

92
00:03:18,740 --> 00:03:20,100
I want to say half the time.

93
00:03:20,100 --> 00:03:22,260
Half the time I turn down insurance.

94
00:03:22,260 --> 00:03:24,240
They ask me whether I got a deductible and try

95
00:03:24,240 --> 00:03:25,000
to sell me that way.

96
00:03:25,000 --> 00:03:27,660
And I assume the reason why they use that sales pitch is

97
00:03:27,660 --> 00:03:29,340
because it's the most effective one they got.

98
00:03:30,580 --> 00:03:32,700
People are saying, you realize, so this way,

99
00:03:32,880 --> 00:03:34,760
if you could crash the car, destroy it,

100
00:03:34,760 --> 00:03:38,120
and you walk away, you don't know what to do.

101
00:03:38,120 --> 00:03:38,360
All right.

102
00:03:38,360 --> 00:03:40,740
But I could have bought a policy like that already,

103
00:03:40,740 --> 00:03:41,520
and I didn't want it.

104
00:03:41,900 --> 00:03:43,180
And why didn't you want it before?

105
00:03:43,180 --> 00:03:44,480
Because it's more expensive.

106
00:03:45,020 --> 00:03:45,660
And you know what?

107
00:03:45,660 --> 00:03:48,580
Those, the policy that you're paying to the auto insurance

108
00:03:48,580 --> 00:03:51,360
or to the rental car company is way more expensive per day

109
00:03:51,360 --> 00:03:52,220
than an oil policy.

110
00:03:52,880 --> 00:03:54,340
So it's especially crazy to do it.

111
00:03:54,340 --> 00:03:57,400
And on top of it, does it really, does this really get rid

112
00:03:57,400 --> 00:03:59,760
of all the risk of an auto accident, a rental car,

113
00:04:00,080 --> 00:04:02,320
if you have a policy that covers all damages?

114
00:04:03,680 --> 00:04:04,320
You could die.

115
00:04:04,720 --> 00:04:05,600
You could be maimed.

116
00:04:06,000 --> 00:04:07,040
They only have everybody to sell.

117
00:04:07,200 --> 00:04:09,340
They aren't even really actually moving the risk

118
00:04:09,340 --> 00:04:10,560
down from one to zero.

119
00:04:10,900 --> 00:04:12,680
But they try to create the illusion of it

120
00:04:12,680 --> 00:04:14,800
because people love that idea so much.

121
00:04:14,800 --> 00:04:14,960
All right.

122
00:04:14,960 --> 00:04:20,880
All right.

123
00:04:20,880 --> 00:04:21,320
Yes, yes.

124
00:04:21,600 --> 00:04:22,640
Although, you know, the very fact

125
00:04:22,640 --> 00:04:25,040
that they are selling it to you suggests they've got some

126
00:04:25,040 --> 00:04:26,600
actuaries and calculated the moral hazard

127
00:04:26,600 --> 00:04:29,380
and they determine it's not that, no big deal at all compared

128
00:04:29,380 --> 00:04:32,100
to the very high price per day that they are charging you.

129
00:04:33,680 --> 00:04:34,000
All right.

130
00:04:34,060 --> 00:04:34,700
So moving on.

131
00:04:35,900 --> 00:04:36,080
All right.

132
00:04:36,420 --> 00:04:39,240
One of the biggest results in behavioral economics

133
00:04:39,240 --> 00:04:42,160
and one that behavioral economists never stop talking

134
00:04:42,160 --> 00:04:45,020
about, hardly ever stop talking about,

135
00:04:45,020 --> 00:04:47,920
is that people are loss averse.

136
00:04:48,260 --> 00:04:48,860
Loss averse.

137
00:04:49,640 --> 00:04:49,880
All right.

138
00:04:49,880 --> 00:04:51,600
Now you may say, well isn't everyone loss averse?

139
00:04:52,280 --> 00:04:53,740
Not in this technical sense.

140
00:04:54,100 --> 00:04:54,960
Not in this technical sense.

141
00:04:55,780 --> 00:04:59,800
So in particular, in basic consumer theory,

142
00:05:00,480 --> 00:05:02,420
preferences and endowments are totally separate.

143
00:05:02,860 --> 00:05:05,720
So you have a utility function which doesn't have any reference

144
00:05:05,720 --> 00:05:07,720
to what, to your initial possessions.

145
00:05:08,840 --> 00:05:09,140
All right.

146
00:05:09,140 --> 00:05:12,860
But the idea of loss aversion, again in this technical sense,

147
00:05:13,340 --> 00:05:16,460
is that losing something that you started with is worse

148
00:05:16,460 --> 00:05:17,980
than never having had it at all.

149
00:05:19,040 --> 00:05:20,380
That's the way loss aversion works.

150
00:05:20,760 --> 00:05:23,180
So if you get a box of candy for your birthday and then you drop

151
00:05:23,180 --> 00:05:24,900
it in the mud, you'll feel worse

152
00:05:24,900 --> 00:05:25,820
than if you didn't get a present.

153
00:05:26,980 --> 00:05:27,620
That's the idea.

154
00:05:28,200 --> 00:05:29,600
You know, if you have an, if you,

155
00:05:29,600 --> 00:05:31,760
if your car gets a nice new paint job

156
00:05:31,760 --> 00:05:33,900
and then it gets scratched, you feel worse

157
00:05:34,000 --> 00:05:36,320
than if you just didn't get the paint job

158
00:05:36,320 --> 00:05:39,500
and the car was scratched all along.

159
00:05:40,820 --> 00:05:40,980
All right.

160
00:05:40,980 --> 00:05:41,940
So that's the idea.

161
00:05:41,940 --> 00:05:44,500
And again, so loss doesn't mean that you don't,

162
00:05:44,500 --> 00:05:46,500
it means that you don't like losing things

163
00:05:46,500 --> 00:05:47,280
that you currently have.

164
00:05:48,020 --> 00:05:52,440
In other words, your utility depends in part upon the match

165
00:05:52,440 --> 00:05:54,160
between what you currently have

166
00:05:54,360 --> 00:05:56,440
and what you think you ought to have or what you started with.

167
00:05:58,380 --> 00:06:00,180
Okay. Which is again a pretty intuitive idea.

168
00:06:00,180 --> 00:06:02,640
And if you know small children, this makes perfect sense.

169
00:06:03,180 --> 00:06:05,240
Because, you know, as I think I mentioned,

170
00:06:05,240 --> 00:06:09,360
but worth repeating, if you want a child to throw a tantrum,

171
00:06:09,660 --> 00:06:12,120
a great thing to do is to give them something nice

172
00:06:12,340 --> 00:06:14,860
and then let them play with it for an hour and then say, okay,

173
00:06:14,860 --> 00:06:15,640
it's mine again.

174
00:06:15,640 --> 00:06:16,420
Now it's mine again.

175
00:06:16,420 --> 00:06:18,100
You lose it, you know, I'm taking it away from you.

176
00:06:18,100 --> 00:06:21,220
And that kid will be way more miserable

177
00:06:21,440 --> 00:06:23,340
than if the child had never given the thing.

178
00:06:24,560 --> 00:06:26,520
If you want to reduce the risk of this,

179
00:06:26,520 --> 00:06:28,600
there's no guarantees here, but if you want to reduce the risk,

180
00:06:28,980 --> 00:06:31,800
you very clearly tell the child, this is my toy.

181
00:06:32,040 --> 00:06:34,340
It belongs to me and to me alone.

182
00:06:34,340 --> 00:06:39,460
But I'm going to be nice and allow you, a non-owner

183
00:06:39,460 --> 00:06:43,440
of this good, to play with it for a small amount of time.

184
00:06:44,260 --> 00:06:45,580
Then I'm going to ask for it back.

185
00:06:45,900 --> 00:06:47,100
You must give it back to me.

186
00:06:47,380 --> 00:06:49,360
If you complain, I will never give you anything

187
00:06:49,360 --> 00:06:49,980
like this again.

188
00:06:50,700 --> 00:06:53,900
Right? And then you reduce your risk of a tantrum from like 99%

189
00:06:54,340 --> 00:06:55,920
down to maybe 40%.

190
00:06:56,700 --> 00:06:56,960
All right?

191
00:06:57,400 --> 00:07:01,480
So it's a big game, but still, like the whole sales pitch is

192
00:07:01,480 --> 00:07:04,940
to try to convince the person that though they are playing

193
00:07:04,940 --> 00:07:06,940
with the toy, it isn't their toy.

194
00:07:07,540 --> 00:07:07,740
Yes?

195
00:07:07,980 --> 00:07:09,440
This is one of the techniques of the stoics.

196
00:07:09,840 --> 00:07:14,380
Good, yes, great analogy, the stoics and Epicure.

197
00:07:14,580 --> 00:07:15,900
Things that are not in your control,

198
00:07:16,180 --> 00:07:17,540
you identify as not your own.

199
00:07:18,020 --> 00:07:18,580
Right, right, yes.

200
00:07:18,580 --> 00:07:21,480
So if you have a cup that you love, you say, this is my cup.

201
00:07:21,820 --> 00:07:22,940
It's going to break at some point.

202
00:07:23,940 --> 00:07:24,640
Yeah, that's very good.

203
00:07:24,640 --> 00:07:25,440
It's just here for a time.

204
00:07:25,440 --> 00:07:28,440
And it's also very much in the philosophy of Epicurus.

205
00:07:29,060 --> 00:07:30,440
So today, Epicure means someone

206
00:07:30,440 --> 00:07:32,440
who really appreciates fine wine and stuff like that.

207
00:07:32,440 --> 00:07:34,200
But Epicurus was nothing like that at all.

208
00:07:34,580 --> 00:07:36,320
Epicurus was someone who advised everyone

209
00:07:36,320 --> 00:07:38,480
to lower their expectations to the minimum level

210
00:07:38,780 --> 00:07:40,300
so they'd be happy all the time.

211
00:07:41,120 --> 00:07:43,840
Like every day, basically you expect, well,

212
00:07:43,840 --> 00:07:45,820
every day you say, well, look, my expectation is

213
00:07:45,820 --> 00:07:47,160
to not be horribly tortured today.

214
00:07:47,780 --> 00:07:48,960
And every day it doesn't happen.

215
00:07:48,960 --> 00:07:50,520
You're like, hey, this is fantastic.

216
00:07:51,720 --> 00:07:54,680
It's all like I got way more than not being horribly tortured.

217
00:07:54,680 --> 00:07:55,720
I had meals.

218
00:07:56,000 --> 00:07:57,420
I had a conversation with friends.

219
00:07:57,420 --> 00:07:58,720
I went for a pleasant stroll.

220
00:07:58,720 --> 00:08:00,940
And so we get the same idea

221
00:08:00,940 --> 00:08:03,880
where if you just make your expectations really low,

222
00:08:04,120 --> 00:08:04,980
then they're easily met.

223
00:08:04,980 --> 00:08:08,340
And then so much of happiness is not about absolute levels.

224
00:08:08,340 --> 00:08:10,720
It's about disparities between what you think you're entitled

225
00:08:10,720 --> 00:08:11,820
to and what you've actually got.

226
00:08:14,180 --> 00:08:15,620
Okay. All right.

227
00:08:15,620 --> 00:08:21,780
Anyway, this insight that people are loss aversion is the root

228
00:08:21,820 --> 00:08:24,300
of the main theoretical innovation

229
00:08:24,300 --> 00:08:25,260
of behavioral economics.

230
00:08:26,000 --> 00:08:26,220
All right.

231
00:08:26,280 --> 00:08:27,060
What do I mean by this?

232
00:08:27,060 --> 00:08:28,440
I mean a new theory.

233
00:08:28,440 --> 00:08:31,620
Normally, behavioral economists are just people poking holes

234
00:08:31,620 --> 00:08:33,360
in the standard theory and saying, you know,

235
00:08:33,360 --> 00:08:34,560
that theory doesn't work.

236
00:08:34,560 --> 00:08:36,760
Ha ha. And there's another theory that doesn't work.

237
00:08:36,760 --> 00:08:37,300
And another one.

238
00:08:37,300 --> 00:08:38,880
It's got five different holes.

239
00:08:39,440 --> 00:08:39,740
All right.

240
00:08:39,740 --> 00:08:42,100
Now, it's easy to sit around poking holes in theories, right?

241
00:08:42,620 --> 00:08:44,740
But much harder to come up with a new theory

242
00:08:44,740 --> 00:08:45,960
that accurately describes the world.

243
00:08:47,140 --> 00:08:49,920
Normally, behavioral economists just realize how hard that's

244
00:08:49,920 --> 00:08:50,860
going to be if they don't do it.

245
00:08:51,580 --> 00:08:55,280
But there is a theory that behavioral economists are well

246
00:08:55,280 --> 00:08:57,140
associated with, which is called prospect theory,

247
00:08:57,140 --> 00:09:02,540
where they actually do try to explain the data that they got.

248
00:09:02,540 --> 00:09:05,500
And we're going to see how successful they are.

249
00:09:06,720 --> 00:09:07,460
All right.

250
00:09:07,680 --> 00:09:08,780
So prospect theory.

251
00:09:13,820 --> 00:09:14,080
All right.

252
00:09:15,140 --> 00:09:18,220
So the idea of prospect theory is that we get rid

253
00:09:18,220 --> 00:09:22,600
of this regular old expected utility curve.

254
00:09:22,600 --> 00:09:26,920
And notice, does that look kind of like that one?

255
00:09:28,260 --> 00:09:28,900
Yes. All right.

256
00:09:28,900 --> 00:09:31,320
So this is your usual risk-averse curve,

257
00:09:31,740 --> 00:09:35,140
where if you go and draw a line between any two points,

258
00:09:35,640 --> 00:09:37,200
the curve is above the lines,

259
00:09:37,500 --> 00:09:40,160
indicating that people prefer a sure thing to a gamma.

260
00:09:41,380 --> 00:09:44,780
Right? Indicating that if you could have the expected value

261
00:09:45,360 --> 00:09:50,020
of this chord here, you would rather have the expected value

262
00:09:50,020 --> 00:09:53,400
rather than take a probability of a certain chance of that,

263
00:09:53,400 --> 00:09:55,140
a certain chance of that, which is the same average value.

264
00:09:56,800 --> 00:09:57,840
Okay? All right.

265
00:09:57,840 --> 00:09:58,980
Now, that's the normal thing.

266
00:09:59,440 --> 00:10:02,800
What prospect theory does is get rid of that curve and replace it

267
00:10:02,800 --> 00:10:04,360
with an NS-shaped curve.

268
00:10:05,700 --> 00:10:08,160
So that's classic prospect theory.

269
00:10:10,900 --> 00:10:12,680
What's so special about this curve?

270
00:10:13,340 --> 00:10:13,660
All right.

271
00:10:13,700 --> 00:10:18,780
Well, the idea here is that that is like your status quo point.

272
00:10:18,780 --> 00:10:21,660
That is, so that point there is the status quo point.

273
00:10:21,980 --> 00:10:24,080
So that's where you start off.

274
00:10:24,400 --> 00:10:26,700
That's sort of like the amount of wealth that you take

275
00:10:26,700 --> 00:10:28,300
for granted that you feel entitled to.

276
00:10:28,900 --> 00:10:34,320
And then what it says is that for amounts of wealth above what

277
00:10:34,320 --> 00:10:36,380
you take for granted, then you have normal risk aversion.

278
00:10:37,000 --> 00:10:39,140
Right? You see how it is that same concave shape?

279
00:10:40,380 --> 00:10:43,700
So it says that if you have a chance to get extra stuff,

280
00:10:44,300 --> 00:10:45,800
if you have a chance of getting more stuff,

281
00:10:45,800 --> 00:10:48,060
then you're risk-averse, and then you'd rather be certain

282
00:10:48,060 --> 00:10:50,860
of getting the extra stuff, of getting an average

283
00:10:50,860 --> 00:10:54,100
of the possibilities to take your chances.

284
00:10:54,880 --> 00:10:59,160
But below the status quo point, below this point

285
00:10:59,240 --> 00:11:01,660
where you define your expectations,

286
00:11:02,200 --> 00:11:03,180
that is the other way around.

287
00:11:03,920 --> 00:11:06,940
Here, at this point, you'd rather actually take the gamble.

288
00:11:08,260 --> 00:11:09,640
You'd rather actually take the gamble.

289
00:11:09,640 --> 00:11:14,640
So this says that if there is, that if you're, if there's a,

290
00:11:14,640 --> 00:11:17,780
if you're going to lose money, you would rather go

291
00:11:17,780 --> 00:11:20,440
and take a gamble where you have a chance of losing a ton of money

292
00:11:20,440 --> 00:11:22,280
but another possibility of not losing anything,

293
00:11:22,720 --> 00:11:26,520
versus just taking a, an average loss for sure.

294
00:11:28,640 --> 00:11:33,940
So in prospect theory, you are risk-averse above the loss

295
00:11:33,940 --> 00:11:37,760
point, but you are risk-preferring below the

296
00:11:37,760 --> 00:11:38,280
loss point.

297
00:11:40,000 --> 00:11:40,340
All right.

298
00:11:41,200 --> 00:11:44,560
So what's the point of this?

299
00:11:44,560 --> 00:11:47,920
Well, this is to say things like, well, sure,

300
00:11:48,200 --> 00:11:52,340
when someone is going, has a chance of getting a game,

301
00:11:52,640 --> 00:11:54,260
then they're going to want insurance,

302
00:11:54,260 --> 00:11:55,220
then they want certainty.

303
00:11:55,460 --> 00:12:00,020
On the other hand, for a loss, people will take big chances

304
00:12:00,020 --> 00:12:01,600
because they don't want to lose anything.

305
00:12:02,280 --> 00:12:04,020
Right? And it's really important not to lose anything,

306
00:12:04,020 --> 00:12:07,400
so they would take, you know, so you'd either lose $1,000

307
00:12:07,400 --> 00:12:10,440
for sure, or you could have a 50% chance of losing $3,000

308
00:12:10,440 --> 00:12:11,980
and a 50% chance of losing nothing.

309
00:12:11,980 --> 00:12:16,000
And someone with, who satisfies this prospect theory,

310
00:12:16,260 --> 00:12:17,520
would like that second gamble.

311
00:12:17,860 --> 00:12:18,520
They'd be interested.

312
00:12:19,340 --> 00:12:20,860
He's like, so then there's a 50% chance,

313
00:12:20,860 --> 00:12:23,460
what 50% chance I won't lose anything at all?

314
00:12:23,700 --> 00:12:25,340
Awesome. Okay, I want to take that.

315
00:12:25,340 --> 00:12:26,880
And then, of course, they really don't like it

316
00:12:26,880 --> 00:12:29,580
when they lose $3,000, but still, they want the gamble

317
00:12:29,580 --> 00:12:31,580
where they have a 50% chance of not losing anything.

318
00:12:33,080 --> 00:12:33,340
All right.

319
00:12:34,120 --> 00:12:38,200
Now, this is the simple version of prospect theory.

320
00:12:38,680 --> 00:12:41,060
Now, if you're thinking about it for a little while,

321
00:12:41,060 --> 00:12:43,820
and again, this is the kind of thing where people came

322
00:12:43,820 --> 00:12:46,940
up with this really smart, and it takes so little time to come

323
00:12:46,940 --> 00:12:48,740
up with seemingly devastating objections

324
00:12:49,380 --> 00:12:51,280
that you wonder what's going on,

325
00:12:51,280 --> 00:12:52,640
and I don't have any good story for that.

326
00:12:53,080 --> 00:12:56,080
But let's just take a look at this curve.

327
00:12:56,160 --> 00:12:58,300
Prospect theory, supposedly the great theory

328
00:12:58,300 --> 00:13:00,420
that shows how wrong expected utility theory is.

329
00:13:00,920 --> 00:13:04,120
And prospect theory says that people risk a person gains,

330
00:13:04,460 --> 00:13:05,840
but risk preferring over losses.

331
00:13:06,380 --> 00:13:08,520
And therefore, what do you want to,

332
00:13:08,520 --> 00:13:10,180
well, when do you want to buy insurance?

333
00:13:10,180 --> 00:13:11,360
Do you want to buy insurance for gains

334
00:13:11,360 --> 00:13:12,860
or losses according to prospect theory?

335
00:13:13,740 --> 00:13:16,300
You want to buy insurance for gains.

336
00:13:17,100 --> 00:13:17,540
All right.

337
00:13:17,900 --> 00:13:20,260
Now, let's think about all known forms of insurance.

338
00:13:21,780 --> 00:13:23,760
Fire insurance, is that for gains or losses?

339
00:13:23,960 --> 00:13:26,360
Auto insurance, gains or losses?

340
00:13:26,360 --> 00:13:28,380
Health insurance, gains or losses?

341
00:13:28,980 --> 00:13:30,600
Life insurance, gains or losses?

342
00:13:31,260 --> 00:13:33,660
Every kind of insurance that I've ever heard of and bought

343
00:13:33,660 --> 00:13:37,040
like virtually is actually for the kind

344
00:13:37,040 --> 00:13:41,420
that prospect theory says people don't want insurance for.

345
00:13:41,660 --> 00:13:42,080
All right.

346
00:13:42,580 --> 00:13:45,700
And then on top of it, there's also another puzzle.

347
00:13:45,840 --> 00:13:48,140
How about gambling, casinos, lotteries?

348
00:13:48,500 --> 00:13:49,100
What are those?

349
00:13:50,520 --> 00:13:53,380
Gains. And what are people doing when they go to Las Vegas

350
00:13:53,380 --> 00:13:55,100
or when they buy a lottery ticket?

351
00:13:56,120 --> 00:13:58,940
Is they are acting risk preferring because, of course,

352
00:13:58,940 --> 00:14:01,700
the reason those casinos exist is because they don't pay

353
00:14:02,180 --> 00:14:03,680
as much on average as you put in.

354
00:14:03,680 --> 00:14:07,120
So far from being a more beautiful theory,

355
00:14:07,120 --> 00:14:09,920
it just creates two giant new categories of error.

356
00:14:11,040 --> 00:14:15,860
So it's weird that anyone ever found this remotely plausible

357
00:14:15,860 --> 00:14:16,480
to me anyway.

358
00:14:16,480 --> 00:14:18,900
I don't know why it ever had any appeal.

359
00:14:18,900 --> 00:14:20,000
All right.

360
00:14:20,000 --> 00:14:21,660
Now, I'm a crock.

361
00:14:21,660 --> 00:14:23,740
Now, of course, I'm not the only person to notice this.

362
00:14:24,280 --> 00:14:28,100
And as a result of this total and humiliating failure

363
00:14:28,100 --> 00:14:31,380
of simple prospect theory, there is advanced prospect theory.

364
00:14:31,380 --> 00:14:32,800
All right.

365
00:14:32,800 --> 00:14:35,020
Advanced prospect theory does get rid

366
00:14:35,020 --> 00:14:37,000
of these problems in the following way.

367
00:14:41,000 --> 00:14:41,220
All right.

368
00:14:41,220 --> 00:14:43,120
It says, all right, look, we are wrong to try

369
00:14:43,120 --> 00:14:44,600
to make this a two-dimensional model,

370
00:14:44,600 --> 00:14:46,600
to make this a simple model here

371
00:14:46,600 --> 00:14:49,700
where there's just a set of square point above and below.

372
00:14:50,000 --> 00:14:52,580
Really, what we need is what is called the four-fold theory.

373
00:14:53,340 --> 00:14:54,240
The four-fold theory.

374
00:14:54,800 --> 00:14:55,020
All right.

375
00:14:55,020 --> 00:14:57,860
The four-fold theory says that people are all the following.

376
00:14:57,860 --> 00:15:01,660
First of all, risk aversion gains with high probabilities.

377
00:15:02,580 --> 00:15:05,800
So this says, for example, that people, that, you know,

378
00:15:05,800 --> 00:15:09,780
that if you are coming into an inheritance, say,

379
00:15:10,000 --> 00:15:12,020
people would go and buy insurance to make sure

380
00:15:12,020 --> 00:15:14,200
that policy where they have a 90% chance

381
00:15:14,200 --> 00:15:15,540
of becomes 100% chance.

382
00:15:16,440 --> 00:15:19,820
So basically, people will pay to, you know, will pay to get rid

383
00:15:19,820 --> 00:15:22,020
of a small risk so that they know they're getting a gain

384
00:15:22,020 --> 00:15:22,340
for sure.

385
00:15:22,340 --> 00:15:23,500
All right.

386
00:15:23,500 --> 00:15:24,100
So let's go do that.

387
00:15:24,600 --> 00:15:26,680
It says people are risking gains with low probabilities.

388
00:15:26,680 --> 00:15:28,800
So this says that people will buy lottery tickets

389
00:15:28,800 --> 00:15:29,640
and go to Las Vegas.

390
00:15:30,380 --> 00:15:30,640
All right.

391
00:15:30,640 --> 00:15:32,780
Then, they're risk averse and loss of low probabilities,

392
00:15:32,780 --> 00:15:34,240
so that's why they buy normal insurance.

393
00:15:34,240 --> 00:15:37,140
And then finally, they're risk seeking and loss

394
00:15:37,140 --> 00:15:40,640
of high probabilities, so that's why, say,

395
00:15:40,640 --> 00:15:47,220
people might pretend that their car was stolen rather

396
00:15:47,220 --> 00:15:48,160
than admit that they crashed it

397
00:15:48,160 --> 00:15:49,260
so the ride doesn't get mad at them.

398
00:15:50,000 --> 00:15:52,100
Right? So like, well, it's pretty likely that she's going

399
00:15:52,100 --> 00:15:55,340
to go and get mad at me if I admit that I crashed the car.

400
00:15:55,340 --> 00:15:57,940
So I'll make up a lie, which actually is probably going

401
00:15:57,940 --> 00:16:01,160
to fail too, but I'm just going to go and take this chance

402
00:16:01,160 --> 00:16:04,260
of completely escaping all dissatisfaction.

403
00:16:05,060 --> 00:16:05,320
All right.

404
00:16:06,140 --> 00:16:07,920
So that is the fourfold theory.

405
00:16:07,920 --> 00:16:09,080
It's gotten a lot of attention.

406
00:16:09,500 --> 00:16:11,940
Now, remember, this is supposed

407
00:16:11,940 --> 00:16:13,460
to be an empirically testable theory.

408
00:16:13,460 --> 00:16:15,640
Now, when you look at this fourfold,

409
00:16:15,640 --> 00:16:17,080
let's look at the simple one.

410
00:16:17,380 --> 00:16:18,720
That one is not so hard to figure

411
00:16:18,720 --> 00:16:20,080
out how you test empirically, right?

412
00:16:21,460 --> 00:16:23,880
Because it says, look, people are risk preferring

413
00:16:23,880 --> 00:16:28,120
if they're in games and they are risk aversing games,

414
00:16:28,120 --> 00:16:30,040
risk preferring losses, then all you need

415
00:16:30,040 --> 00:16:32,460
to know is what their status quo is.

416
00:16:34,100 --> 00:16:35,040
So where do they start?

417
00:16:35,500 --> 00:16:37,900
And then you know, and then we look at what happens

418
00:16:37,900 --> 00:16:39,340
when a change happens, and it's pretty simple.

419
00:16:40,180 --> 00:16:44,440
But for this fourfold theory, we need to know what counts

420
00:16:44,440 --> 00:16:48,960
as not only gain or loss, but we also need to go

421
00:16:48,960 --> 00:16:54,380
and define the cutoffs for high versus low probability.

422
00:16:54,380 --> 00:16:56,980
And notice, from the description,

423
00:16:56,980 --> 00:17:00,160
I would say in a practice, outside of a lab,

424
00:17:00,160 --> 00:17:02,740
it's pretty easy for someone to just call something high

425
00:17:02,740 --> 00:17:05,040
or low depending upon how they need it to work out.

426
00:17:05,840 --> 00:17:08,180
Right? So like is a 20% chance of a loss a high

427
00:17:08,180 --> 00:17:09,340
or low probability of a loss?

428
00:17:11,000 --> 00:17:11,580
I don't know.

429
00:17:11,860 --> 00:17:14,020
I haven't seen anyone who likes prospect theory go

430
00:17:14,020 --> 00:17:17,800
and say what the cut point is for which theory applies.

431
00:17:17,800 --> 00:17:20,440
So this actually is much closer

432
00:17:20,440 --> 00:17:21,800
to a theory that explains everything.

433
00:17:23,180 --> 00:17:25,140
In a lab, of course, you could set it up to make sure

434
00:17:25,140 --> 00:17:28,480
so that there's one probability is higher than the other one.

435
00:17:28,760 --> 00:17:33,060
So if someone is risk averse in one probability,

436
00:17:33,060 --> 00:17:34,300
then they better be risk averse

437
00:17:34,300 --> 00:17:36,320
in an even higher probability, for example.

438
00:17:36,320 --> 00:17:37,560
So you can do that in a lab.

439
00:17:37,560 --> 00:17:39,280
But in the real world, what does this really say?

440
00:17:39,280 --> 00:17:41,620
I mean, to me, it's just sometimes people risk averse,

441
00:17:41,620 --> 00:17:42,600
sometimes they risk preferring.

442
00:17:43,460 --> 00:17:45,480
And I don't see that it adds that much more.

443
00:17:45,480 --> 00:17:45,660
All right.

444
00:17:45,660 --> 00:17:48,640
All right.

445
00:17:48,980 --> 00:17:49,720
Questions?

446
00:17:49,800 --> 00:17:50,580
Questions?

447
00:17:51,100 --> 00:17:51,540
All right.

448
00:17:52,440 --> 00:17:54,500
Then last topic, intertemporal anomalies.

449
00:17:55,120 --> 00:17:55,420
All right.

450
00:17:55,420 --> 00:17:57,020
So remember current capital markets?

451
00:17:57,020 --> 00:17:57,900
All right.

452
00:17:57,900 --> 00:17:58,580
I'm going to ask Scott Burdick.

453
00:17:58,580 --> 00:17:59,660
I don't know if you remember current capital markets.

454
00:17:59,660 --> 00:17:59,760
Sure.

455
00:17:59,760 --> 00:17:59,820
Yes.

456
00:17:59,820 --> 00:18:16,880
I have a question actually.

457
00:18:16,880 --> 00:18:19,460
Like we were saying that if you were

458
00:18:19,460 --> 00:18:21,220
to test the simple prospect theory,

459
00:18:21,220 --> 00:18:22,780
isn't that like the status quo point

460
00:18:22,780 --> 00:18:23,940
for each different individual?

461
00:18:23,940 --> 00:18:25,380
Like they're also like very subjective.

462
00:18:25,380 --> 00:18:25,980
You can't really.

463
00:18:25,980 --> 00:18:26,820
That is another good point.

464
00:18:26,820 --> 00:18:27,580
So again, of course, in a lab,

465
00:18:27,580 --> 00:18:28,880
you can just say the status quo is whatever amount

466
00:18:28,880 --> 00:18:29,380
of money we start.

467
00:18:29,380 --> 00:18:31,860
But I would say that with, you know, certainly with kids,

468
00:18:31,860 --> 00:18:34,220
like with younger kids, I have a very good sense

469
00:18:34,220 --> 00:18:37,340
of what they consider their starting point.

470
00:18:37,780 --> 00:18:40,100
So, you know, like if they've never touched a toy before,

471
00:18:40,100 --> 00:18:41,040
they don't think that's their toy.

472
00:18:41,720 --> 00:18:43,920
If they bring a toy with them to your house,

473
00:18:43,920 --> 00:18:45,880
they think that's their toy.

474
00:18:46,240 --> 00:18:50,020
For adults, I mean, so think about your ability

475
00:18:50,020 --> 00:18:53,520
to predict whether an adult will feel, will say, hey, that's mine.

476
00:18:54,680 --> 00:18:57,140
Or, you know, so I feel like I have a pretty good,

477
00:18:57,140 --> 00:19:00,560
I can make pretty good predictions about that.

478
00:19:00,560 --> 00:19:03,180
And often it will not actually fit legal rules.

479
00:19:03,720 --> 00:19:06,060
So, you know, things like, hey, that's my parking spot.

480
00:19:06,800 --> 00:19:07,800
I don't see a name on it.

481
00:19:08,260 --> 00:19:10,520
And yet people will consider something their parking spot.

482
00:19:10,520 --> 00:19:12,840
And in fact, I think you have a pretty good idea

483
00:19:12,840 --> 00:19:14,560
for when someone will consider a parking spot their

484
00:19:14,560 --> 00:19:15,200
parking spot.

485
00:19:16,700 --> 00:19:18,560
So, I mean, like there is a subjective element.

486
00:19:18,560 --> 00:19:19,500
I don't think it's that hard.

487
00:19:20,180 --> 00:19:21,680
But, like that could just be me.

488
00:19:21,680 --> 00:19:22,520
I could be wrong on that.

489
00:19:22,520 --> 00:19:24,300
So maybe there is more subjectivity.

490
00:19:24,300 --> 00:19:27,320
If it were that subjective, people would be getting

491
00:19:27,320 --> 00:19:30,340
into shouting matches at worst very frequently.

492
00:19:30,680 --> 00:19:33,180
Because if there was some disagreement

493
00:19:33,180 --> 00:19:36,540
about whether the stuff in your wallet is yours, that would be,

494
00:19:36,680 --> 00:19:38,040
like whether that phone is yours.

495
00:19:38,040 --> 00:19:40,540
If I were to say, hey, I don't see your name on this phone.

496
00:19:40,540 --> 00:19:42,540
It's like, it's in the phone.

497
00:19:42,720 --> 00:19:43,360
In the phone?

498
00:19:43,360 --> 00:19:43,980
What does that mean?

499
00:19:43,980 --> 00:19:45,320
It has to be, no, no, no, it has to be engraved

500
00:19:45,320 --> 00:19:46,380
on the phone or it doesn't count.

501
00:19:47,100 --> 00:19:49,060
Right? If I'm like, yeah, I could have these theories.

502
00:19:49,060 --> 00:19:51,520
But I think all, everyone here would disagree with the theory

503
00:19:51,520 --> 00:19:53,760
that your phone must have your name engraved on it

504
00:19:53,760 --> 00:19:55,280
in order for it to be yours.

505
00:19:55,280 --> 00:19:57,240
And otherwise, it's finders keepers.

506
00:19:57,240 --> 00:19:57,420
Right?

507
00:19:57,920 --> 00:19:58,000
Yeah.

508
00:19:58,000 --> 00:19:59,800
You can't actually do that.

509
00:19:59,800 --> 00:20:24,800
You can't.


